WEBVTT

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:02.135
Lesson 2.1.

00:00:02.135 --> 00:00:04.290
DEXs, aggregators and routing.

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:06.165
By the end, you'll choose where to

00:00:06.165 --> 00:00:08.684
swap by comparing what you'd actually receive,

00:00:08.684 --> 00:00:10.345
not the price quoted on screen.

00:00:11.645 --> 00:00:12.935
Here's the plan.

00:00:12.935 --> 00:00:14.127
The two ways a DEX

00:00:14.127 --> 00:00:16.310
can actually price your trade.

00:00:16.310 --> 00:00:18.543
What an aggregator does differently, and

00:00:18.543 --> 00:00:20.695
why routing across pools can help.

00:00:20.695 --> 00:00:23.030
The real formula for what you actually

00:00:23.030 --> 00:00:25.485
receive, once every cost is counted.

00:00:25.485 --> 00:00:27.752
And a full worked example, with real

00:00:27.752 --> 00:00:30.754
numbers, showing exactly when each approach wins.

00:00:32.054 --> 00:00:33.574
Two ways to price a trade.

00:00:34.874 --> 00:00:38.662
First mechanism: the A.M.M. DEX, the one most people

00:00:38.662 --> 00:00:41.735
mean by “DEX.” You trade against a pool of

00:00:41.735 --> 00:00:44.679
two tokens, held by a smart contract.

00:00:44.679 --> 00:00:47.560
A formula, covered fully in Lesson two point two,

00:00:47.560 --> 00:00:50.568
sets the price from the pool's current balance.

00:00:50.568 --> 00:00:53.069
Your trade itself shifts that balance, which

00:00:53.069 --> 00:00:55.148
is exactly what moves the price.

00:00:55.148 --> 00:00:56.780
There's no order book, and no

00:00:56.780 --> 00:00:58.738
waiting for a matching counterparty.

00:01:00.039 --> 00:01:03.020
Second mechanism: the on-chain order book, less

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:05.369
common but worth knowing by name.

00:01:05.369 --> 00:01:07.264
Here's the actual contrast.

00:01:07.264 --> 00:01:10.305
An A.M.M. pool always has a price, set by

00:01:10.305 --> 00:01:14.432
a formula, and always has some liquidity available, even

00:01:14.432 --> 00:01:17.049
if a large trade moves it a lot.

00:01:17.049 --> 00:01:19.386
An order book needs an actual matching

00:01:19.386 --> 00:01:21.603
order at your price, fully or partly

00:01:21.603 --> 00:01:24.239
on-chain, or your trade simply doesn't fill.

00:01:25.539 --> 00:01:27.777
Worth seeing an on-chain order book as

00:01:27.777 --> 00:01:30.301
its own walk-through, since “needs a match”

00:01:30.301 --> 00:01:32.495
undersells how it actually works.

00:01:32.495 --> 00:01:33.751
You place an order, at

00:01:33.751 --> 00:01:35.580
a specific price you choose.

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:38.661
It sits on the book, waiting, visible to everyone,

00:01:38.661 --> 00:01:41.860
fully or partly on-chain depending on the venue.

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:45.310
Another trader's order arrives that matches your price.

00:01:45.310 --> 00:01:48.211
And the trade settles, for exactly your chosen

00:01:48.211 --> 00:01:50.680
price, no impact from pool depth at all.

00:01:51.980 --> 00:01:53.776
One real number, so this isn't

00:01:53.776 --> 00:01:55.525
a niche corner of crypto.

00:01:55.525 --> 00:02:00.709
Industry trackers have recorded decentralised exchanges collectively processing

00:02:00.709 --> 00:02:03.690
tens of billions of dollars in trading volume

00:02:03.690 --> 00:02:06.193
in a typical month, across chains.

00:02:06.193 --> 00:02:09.201
This is outside research, not this program's own

00:02:09.201 --> 00:02:11.780
data, but it's why getting this one habit

00:02:11.780 --> 00:02:15.096
right, comparing net received, matters at real scale,

00:02:15.096 --> 00:02:16.876
not just for your own trades.

00:02:18.176 --> 00:02:19.266
Quick check.

00:02:19.266 --> 00:02:21.921
What actually moves the price in an AMM pool?

00:02:26.341 --> 00:02:29.066
The answer: your own trade does, by shifting

00:02:29.066 --> 00:02:31.730
the pool's balance of the two tokens, which

00:02:31.730 --> 00:02:34.031
is what the pricing formula reacts to.

00:02:35.331 --> 00:02:36.716
Aggregators and routing.

00:02:38.016 --> 00:02:40.634
An aggregator searches many pools and venues

00:02:40.634 --> 00:02:42.621
for your trade, not just one.

00:02:42.621 --> 00:02:45.883
And critically, it may split your order across several

00:02:45.883 --> 00:02:48.670
routes at once, sending part of it through one

00:02:48.670 --> 00:02:51.576
pool and part through another, so no single pool

00:02:51.576 --> 00:02:54.011
absorbs the full price impact alone.

00:02:54.011 --> 00:02:56.129
That splitting is exactly how it can

00:02:56.129 --> 00:02:58.018
land a better net price than any

00:02:58.018 --> 00:03:00.021
single pool could offer on its own.

00:03:01.321 --> 00:03:04.378
Here's what “routing” actually means, step by step,

00:03:04.378 --> 00:03:07.676
since “multi-hop” sounds abstract until you see it.

00:03:07.676 --> 00:03:09.946
A direct route goes straight from token

00:03:09.946 --> 00:03:12.006
A to token B, through one pool.

00:03:12.006 --> 00:03:14.534
A multi-hop route instead goes A to B to

00:03:14.534 --> 00:03:17.362
C, through two or more pools in sequence.

00:03:17.362 --> 00:03:20.642
That extra hop can land a meaningfully better price.

00:03:20.642 --> 00:03:23.118
It also means more contracts your trade

00:03:23.118 --> 00:03:26.152
depends on behaving correctly, one after another.

00:03:27.452 --> 00:03:29.948
Worth being precise about what routing does

00:03:29.948 --> 00:03:33.027
and doesn't guarantee, before the worked example.

00:03:33.027 --> 00:03:35.219
It can land a better price, by spreading

00:03:35.219 --> 00:03:37.497
your trade or finding a cheaper path.

00:03:37.497 --> 00:03:39.790
It cannot make a trade free, and it

00:03:39.790 --> 00:03:43.165
cannot remove price impact entirely, only reduce how

00:03:43.165 --> 00:03:45.567
much of it lands on any one pool.

00:03:45.567 --> 00:03:47.935
Gas for those extra hops is real, and it

00:03:47.935 --> 00:03:50.822
comes out of the same net result you're comparing.

00:03:52.122 --> 00:03:54.353
Before trusting any aggregator with real

00:03:54.353 --> 00:03:57.047
size, here's what's actually worth checking.

00:03:57.047 --> 00:04:00.196
Confirm it's a reputable, widely used aggregator,

00:04:00.196 --> 00:04:03.457
not an unfamiliar interface someone linked you.

00:04:03.457 --> 00:04:05.962
Check that its fee, if any, is disclosed

00:04:05.962 --> 00:04:08.767
plainly, not hidden inside a worse quote.

00:04:08.767 --> 00:04:11.330
And read the route it's proposing, at least

00:04:11.330 --> 00:04:13.777
the token list, before you sign, the same

00:04:13.777 --> 00:04:15.757
habit as reading any other prompt.

00:04:17.057 --> 00:04:19.332
One more honest trade-off worth naming,

00:04:19.332 --> 00:04:22.077
since aggregators aren't purely an upgrade.

00:04:22.077 --> 00:04:25.351
A direct DEX trade touches one contract, so there's

00:04:25.351 --> 00:04:28.737
exactly one thing that needs to behave correctly.

00:04:28.737 --> 00:04:32.170
An aggregator route touches its own routing contract,

00:04:32.170 --> 00:04:34.776
plus every pool along the path, which is

00:04:34.776 --> 00:04:38.336
more surface area, even though each individual contract

00:04:38.336 --> 00:04:41.642
is typically well-audited on a reputable aggregator.

00:04:42.942 --> 00:04:44.032
Quick check.

00:04:44.032 --> 00:04:46.398
Why can splitting an order across several

00:04:46.398 --> 00:04:48.707
pools actually improve the price you get?

00:04:53.127 --> 00:04:56.492
The answer: no single pool absorbs the full trade,

00:04:56.492 --> 00:04:59.526
so no single pool's price moves as far, which

00:04:59.526 --> 00:05:02.297
is exactly what price impact is driven by.

00:05:03.597 --> 00:05:04.702
The real formula.

00:05:06.002 --> 00:05:08.191
Here's the formula, spelled out as four

00:05:08.191 --> 00:05:10.187
steps, not just a line of text.

00:05:10.187 --> 00:05:12.009
Start with the quoted output, the

00:05:12.009 --> 00:05:14.452
headline number the interface shows you.

00:05:14.452 --> 00:05:16.681
Subtract price impact, your own trade

00:05:16.681 --> 00:05:19.092
moving the pool's price against you.

00:05:19.092 --> 00:05:23.132
Subtract pool fees, paid to whoever provided that liquidity.

00:05:23.132 --> 00:05:25.138
And subtract gas, the network fee

00:05:25.138 --> 00:05:27.622
for executing the transaction at all.

00:05:27.622 --> 00:05:30.147
What's left is what actually lands in your wallet.

00:05:31.447 --> 00:05:34.621
Put real numbers on how pool depth changes the

00:05:34.621 --> 00:05:38.066
same trade, since “relative to pool depth” is easy

00:05:38.066 --> 00:05:40.797
to nod along to and hard to picture.

00:05:40.797 --> 00:05:45.040
The same ten-thousand-dollar trade against a deep, million-dollar-plus

00:05:45.040 --> 00:05:47.132
pool might move the price by a few

00:05:47.132 --> 00:05:50.002
hundredths of a percent, barely measurable.

00:05:50.002 --> 00:05:53.888
Against a shallow, ten-thousand-dollar pool, that same trade

00:05:53.888 --> 00:05:56.500
is now a huge share of what's available,

00:05:56.500 --> 00:05:59.303
and price impact can run into double digits.

00:06:00.603 --> 00:06:02.654
Turn that formula into an actual

00:06:02.654 --> 00:06:05.253
pre-trade habit, every time you swap.

00:06:05.253 --> 00:06:07.810
Check the price impact shown, and ask whether

00:06:07.810 --> 00:06:10.223
it's acceptable for a trade this size.

00:06:10.223 --> 00:06:12.717
Confirm the route uses pools and tokens

00:06:12.717 --> 00:06:15.834
you've actually verified, not an unfamiliar token

00:06:15.834 --> 00:06:17.693
that appeared in a route.

00:06:17.693 --> 00:06:20.830
And set your slippage tolerance on purpose, covered

00:06:20.830 --> 00:06:23.304
fully in Lesson two point five, not left

00:06:23.304 --> 00:06:25.778
on whatever default the interface picked.

00:06:27.078 --> 00:06:28.083
Worked example.

00:06:29.383 --> 00:06:34.195
Here's the real comparison, selling 20 ETH.

00:06:34.195 --> 00:06:38.898
A single pool, direct, quotes 59,100 USDC,

00:06:38.898 --> 00:06:42.698
costs $8 in gas, netting 59,092.

00:06:42.698 --> 00:06:48.040
An aggregator, splitting across 3 pools, quotes

00:06:48.040 --> 00:06:52.903
59,420, costs $20 in gas, netting 59,400.

00:06:52.903 --> 00:06:56.064
The aggregator wins, by roughly $308,

00:06:56.064 --> 00:06:58.642
even after its higher gas cost.

00:06:59.942 --> 00:07:02.048
Now the same comparison, at a much

00:07:02.048 --> 00:07:05.117
smaller size, since the answer actually flips.

00:07:05.117 --> 00:07:08.621
Selling zero point five E.T.H., the aggregator quotes

00:07:08.621 --> 00:07:11.647
three dollars better than the direct pool.

00:07:11.647 --> 00:07:14.272
But it also costs twelve dollars more in gas.

00:07:14.272 --> 00:07:17.235
Net, the direct pool wins by nine dollars.

00:07:17.235 --> 00:07:19.390
Big trades benefit from routing.

00:07:19.390 --> 00:07:21.985
Small trades mostly lose to the extra gas.

00:07:23.285 --> 00:07:25.771
Here's how to actually run this comparison

00:07:25.771 --> 00:07:28.500
yourself, on any trade, before you commit.

00:07:28.500 --> 00:07:31.175
Get a quote from a single DEX, direct.

00:07:31.175 --> 00:07:33.195
Get a quote from an aggregator, for

00:07:33.195 --> 00:07:35.123
the exact same pair and size.

00:07:35.123 --> 00:07:36.850
Subtract each route's own gas

00:07:36.850 --> 00:07:38.993
estimate from its quoted output.

00:07:38.993 --> 00:07:41.379
And take whichever net number is higher, not

00:07:41.379 --> 00:07:43.553
whichever quote looked better on its own.

00:07:44.853 --> 00:07:45.943
Quick check.

00:07:45.943 --> 00:07:48.222
Why does the direct pool win on

00:07:48.222 --> 00:07:50.643
the small 0.5 ETH trade, when the

00:07:50.643 --> 00:07:52.993
aggregator quoted a better price?

00:07:57.413 --> 00:08:00.713
The answer: the aggregator's extra gas cost, twelve

00:08:00.713 --> 00:08:04.583
dollars, was larger than its three-dollar price improvement,

00:08:04.583 --> 00:08:07.058
so net, the direct pool came out ahead.

00:08:08.358 --> 00:08:09.818
Here's your checklist.

00:08:09.818 --> 00:08:12.233
Do it now, for real, on every swap.

00:08:12.233 --> 00:08:14.305
You compare net output after gas,

00:08:14.305 --> 00:08:16.433
not the headline quoted price.

00:08:16.433 --> 00:08:18.332
Price impact shown is acceptable

00:08:18.332 --> 00:08:20.243
for the size you're trading.

00:08:20.243 --> 00:08:22.238
Your route uses reputable pools

00:08:22.238 --> 00:08:24.783
and tokens you've actually verified.

00:08:24.783 --> 00:08:27.505
And your slippage tolerance is set on purpose,

00:08:27.505 --> 00:08:29.763
covered fully in Lesson two point five.

00:08:31.063 --> 00:08:33.131
One last thing worth naming before the

00:08:33.131 --> 00:08:35.411
recap, since it sets up everything through

00:08:35.411 --> 00:08:36.983
the rest of this module.

00:08:36.983 --> 00:08:40.080
Every number in this lesson, price impact, the

00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:43.111
formula, the worked example, comes out of the

00:08:43.111 --> 00:08:46.903
same underlying pricing formula a pool actually runs.

00:08:46.903 --> 00:08:49.734
Lesson two point two opens that formula up

00:08:49.734 --> 00:08:53.355
directly, so you can calculate these numbers yourself,

00:08:53.355 --> 00:08:55.593
not just recognise them on screen.

00:08:56.893 --> 00:08:58.138
Let's recap.

00:08:58.138 --> 00:09:01.151
An A.M.M. pool always has a price, set by

00:09:01.151 --> 00:09:04.823
a formula; an order book needs an actual match.

00:09:04.823 --> 00:09:07.284
An aggregator can land a better price by

00:09:07.284 --> 00:09:10.104
splitting your order across pools, at the cost

00:09:10.104 --> 00:09:12.265
of more gas for the extra hops.

00:09:12.265 --> 00:09:15.207
The real formula is quoted output, minus

00:09:15.207 --> 00:09:18.090
price impact, minus fees, minus gas.

00:09:18.090 --> 00:09:20.244
And the size of your trade decides

00:09:20.244 --> 00:09:23.012
which approach wins: big trades benefit from

00:09:23.012 --> 00:09:25.535
routing, small trades mostly lose to gas.

00:09:26.835 --> 00:09:28.609
Compare net received, not the

00:09:28.609 --> 00:09:30.565
quote, on every single swap.

00:09:30.565 --> 00:09:33.985
Next up, Lesson 2.2: AMM mathematics.
